            'Present: Still played as a retro variant of the All Fours family.',
          ],
          evolution: "Replaces the 'High, Low, Jack, Game' points of All Fours with a fixed point schedule for specific cards (A, K, Q, J, 10, 5 of trumps).",
          cultural: 'Often played in American south and rural England as a faster, more score-dense version of traditional trick games.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        setup: {
          players: '2 or 3 (individual play).',
          deck: 'Standard 52-card deck.',
          equipment: 'Standard deck, score tracking to 61.',
          dealing: 'Dealer gives 6 cards to each player. The next card is turned up for trumps.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Score 61 points by winning tricks containing the Ace, King, Queen, Jack, 10, or 5 of the trump suit.',
          gameplay: "1. **Trump Determination**: Eldest hand can 'Stand' or 'Beg'. If they beg, the dealer either gifts 1 point to the opponent or deals 3 more cards to each player to find a new trump suit. 2. **Trick Play**: Winner of the lead (or eldest) leads the first trick. Players must follow suit if they can, or they may trump. 3. **Winning**: Highest trump wins. If no trumps, the highest card of the led suit wins. 4. **Point Capture**: Points are ONLY awarded for the following cards IN THE TRUMP SUIT: \n   - **Ten**: 10 points\n   - **Five**: 5 points\n   - **Ace**: 4 points\n   - **King**: 3 points\n   - **Queen**: 2 points\n   - **Jack**: 1 point\n5. **Total**: There are 25 total points available in each hand. The first to reach 61 wins.",
          keyRules: [
            'Only trumps score. Non-trump cards are worth 0 points.',
            "The 'Beg' rule is identical to All Fours: dealer must either gift a point or run the cards (deal 3 more each).",
            'Winning the 10 and the 5 of trumps is the core goal, as they represent 60% of the total points.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'If you hold the 10 or 5 of trumps, do not lead them! Wait until a higher trump (Ace or King) has been played so you can safely drop them on a winning trick.',
          intermediate: 'Begging is more common in All Fives than All Fours because you need a high-point trump suit to score effectively. If the turned-up trump is a suit you are short in, always beg.',
          advanced: "Defensive Trumping: If an opponent leads a high non-trump suit (e.g., Ace of Spades) and you suspect they have the point-trumps, trump early to 'steal' the lead and force them to play their trumps out of order.",
          tips: [
            'The 5 of trumps is a huge prize. It often gets lost in early tricks; keep track of it.',
            'If you have the 10 and the Ace of trumps, you have a guaranteed 14 points if you lead correctly.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        variations: {
          list: [
